---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifePairConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifePairConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="kg-to-pounds-and-ounces">
  <UnitsLifePairConverter client:load spec="kg-to-pounds-and-ounces" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Two numbers, not one</h2>
    <p>
      Birth weights, parcels and small goods are given in pounds and ounces, and the conversion of kg
      to pounds and ounces often goes wrong at the second number. 3.4 kg is 7.4957 pounds. The
      fractional part, 0.4957 lb, is <strong>7.9 ounces</strong>, not 5 ounces and not 49 ounces. To
      get it right, multiply the remainder by sixteen. The composite readout here does that.
    </p>

    <h2>How the split works</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Convert to decimal pounds: 3.4 kg &divide; 0.45359237 = 7.4957 lb.</li>
      <li>Keep the whole pounds: 7 lb.</li>
      <li>Multiply the remainder by 16: 0.4957 &times; 16 = 7.93 oz.</li>
      <li>Round the ounces last, and roll over to another pound if they reach 16.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Naive converters skip that final rollover, and then a form shows "7 lb 16 oz".
    </p>

    <h2>Birth weight reference</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Kilograms</th><th scope="col">Grams</th><th scope="col">Pounds and ounces</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>2.0</td><td>2,000</td><td>4 lb 6.5 oz</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2.5</td><td>2,500</td><td>5 lb 8.2 oz</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3.0</td><td>3,000</td><td>6 lb 9.8 oz</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3.2</td><td>3,200</td><td>7 lb 0.9 oz</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3.4</td><td>3,400</td><td>7 lb 7.9 oz</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3.6</td><td>3,600</td><td>7 lb 15.0 oz</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4.0</td><td>4,000</td><td>8 lb 13.1 oz</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4.5</td><td>4,500</td><td>9 lb 14.7 oz</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The average birth weight in most high-income countries is about 3.3&ndash;3.5 kg, so "seven and
      a half pounds" sounds like the default. The WHO low-birth-weight threshold is 2.5 kg, or
      5 lb 8 oz.
    </p>

    <h2>Parcels and hand luggage</h2>
    <p>
      US courier rate cards step in ounces below a pound and in whole pounds above. European ones step
      in grams. A 500 g parcel is 1 lb 1.6 oz, which prices as a 2 lb item in a whole-pound band. Near a
      boundary, weigh in the unit the carrier prices in. Do not convert a rounded figure.
    </p>

    <h2>This converter goes both ways</h2>
    <p>
      Type into the right-hand box to convert pounds back to kilograms, or press the swap button to
      reverse the pair. The composite pounds-and-ounces readout works in both directions, because it
      comes from the underlying mass, not from the box you typed in.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
